Under CAP/DA, home modifications must be tied to the beneficiary’s assessed needs and plan of care, and they must be the kind of modification that improves the beneficiary’s mobility, safety, independence, accessibility, community integration, or addresses 24/7 coverage concerns. The beneficiary also agrees to work with the multidisciplinary team to identify needs for “equipment, modification, technology, training and education and goods and services.” Effective date in the current policy text I reviewed: September 1, 2025. [[Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)]

## Home modification requirements under CAP/DA

CAP/DA covers home modifications for a beneficiary’s **primary private residence** if the residence is:
- owned by the beneficiary or family;
- rented, if the modifications are portable; or
- rented, if the owner allows the modifications. [[Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)]

Examples of covered home modifications include:
- ramps;
- grab bars, safety rails, and handrails;
- widening doorways or passages for wheelchair or walker access;
- emergency egress when medically necessary because of the responsible party’s physical limitations;
- bathroom modifications for accessibility;
- bedroom modifications to accommodate hospital beds and wheelchairs;
- kitchen accessibility modifications;
- floor coverings for ease of ambulation;
- non-skid surfaces;
- door handle and door modifications;
- lifts and certain other accessibility devices for the home;
- replacement of fixtures, mirrors, and storage space when removed or damaged during an approved modification. [[Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)]

The policy states that equipment, modification, and technology services must be intended to:
- improve, maximize, or enhance mobility, safety, or independence;
- improve community integration;
- improve environmental and community accessibility; or
- address 24-hours-a-day, 7-days-a-week coverage concerns. [[Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)]

## Documentation the Multidisciplinary Team must provide

For equipment/home modification requests, the policy requires documentation including:

- **A recommendation by the MDT** identifying the beneficiary’s need for the requested home modification.  
- The recommendation must **state the cost** of the item or modification required.  
- A **statement of need identified in the service plan**.  
- **Evidence of medical need** recommended by the MDT or by a physician, when applicable.  
- **Shipping costs itemized** in the proposal request.  
- **Other information required for the specific item requested**.  
- If quotes are required for the purchase, adaptation, or modification, **NC Medicaid determines how many quotes are required**.  
- In special circumstances, **NC Medicaid or its designee determines which professionals must make written recommendations**. [[Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)]

The supporting documentation requirements also include:
- a **comprehensive multidisciplinary declaration-of-need assessment** completed by the case manager identifying the equipment, technology, supply, adaptation, or modification needs;
- a **copy of the physician’s order**, when applicable;
- a **recommendation by an appropriate professional** identifying the beneficiary’s need for the requested equipment, supply, adaptation, or modification;
- the **estimated life of the equipment** and the expected length of time the beneficiary will benefit, stated in the request; and
- an **invoice from the supplier** showing the date provided and the cost, with related charges maintained in the CAP Business system. [[Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)]

## Training/documentation requirement

If the request includes equipment or technology related to the modification, the policy also requires:
- a **plan for how the beneficiary and family will be trained** in its use; and
- documentation by the **case manager** that the training was completed and **signed by the CAP/DA beneficiary or responsible party**. [[Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)]

## Bottom line

For CAP/DA home modifications, the MDT must show that the modification is needed for accessibility, safety, independence, or similar waiver-related purposes, that it is tied to the beneficiary’s service plan, and that the request is backed by a formal MDT recommendation plus supporting medical/professional documentation, cost information, and any required quotes or physician orders. [[Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)]

