NC Medicaid does **not** cover ambulance services in these situations:

## General non-covered reasons
Under the Ambulance Services policy, ambulance services are not covered if:

- the beneficiary does not meet the eligibility requirements in Section 2.0;
- the beneficiary does not meet the coverage criteria in Section 3.0;
- the service duplicates another provider’s procedure, product, or service; or
- the service is experimental, investigational, or part of a clinical trial.  
  [Ambulance Services](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/15-ambulance-services/download?attachment) § 4.1 (effective 12/15/2023)

## Ambulance-specific non-covered services
The policy also specifically says NC Medicaid does **not** cover:

- **Loaded mileage to a facility that is not the nearest appropriate facility**;
- an ambulance call where **no treatment is needed**;
- a **false alarm** response;
- situations where the **beneficiary refuses all medical services**;
- ambulance transport for a medical service that is **not itself Medicaid-covered**;
- **commercial airline tickets**;
- **airstrip fees**;
- **taxes** charged separately (local, state, federal, etc.);
- separate additional charges for **nursing personnel** who are employees of a facility or ambulance service;
- **waiting fees**;
- separate charges for **oxygen and other items/supplies**, because those are included in the base rate; and
- **services other than those listed in Section 3.2**.  
  [Ambulance Services](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/15-ambulance-services/download?attachment) §§ 4.2.1.1, 4.2.1.4 (effective 12/15/2023)

## Coverage rule in contrast
The governing coverage section says Medicaid covers emergency ambulance services to the extent necessary to **screen and stabilize** the beneficiary, without prior authorization when a prudent layperson would believe an emergency medical condition exists. So anything outside those covered criteria may fall into the non-covered categories above.  
[Ambulance Services](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/15-ambulance-services/download?attachment) § 3.2.1 (effective 12/15/2023)

I checked for amendments to Policy 15, and the authoritative current document is **Ambulance Services**, effective **December 15, 2023**.

Under NC Medicaid, an HIV case management provider must be certified and must continue to meet staffing, accreditation, compliance, and recertification requirements. A provider can be decertified for a wide range of failures, including billing fraud, inadequate staffing or supervision, documentation failures, confidentiality violations, failure to correct cited deficiencies, and failure to maintain certification requirements. ([Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment))

## Certification requirements

NC Medicaid’s HIV Case Management policy requires the provider to:

- have a documented successful record of **3 years** providing or managing HIV case management programs;
- ensure HIV case management services are provided by **qualified case managers** meeting the policy’s staff qualification standards;
- ensure HIV case managers are supervised by **qualified supervisors** meeting the policy’s supervisor standards;
- enroll **each physical site** with NC Medicaid as an HIV Case Management provider;
- comply with applicable **state and federal Medicaid participation laws**;
- maintain certification as a qualified HIV Case Management provider;
- demonstrate compliance with **initial and ongoing certification processes**;
- demonstrate compliance with monitoring and evaluation of case management records through a **quality improvement plan**;
- allow NC Medicaid or its designated contractor to review beneficiary, operational, and financial records; and
- notify NC Medicaid or its designated contractor of proposed agency changes within **30 calendar days** and no later than **5 business days** after the actual change. ([Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment), § 6.2.1)

In addition, within **1 year of Medicaid enrollment**, the provider must obtain national accreditation from one of the designated accrediting agencies listed in the policy: ACHC, CARF, CHAP, COA, PHAB, The Joint Commission, or URAC. ([Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment), § 6.2.1)

### Staff qualification requirements tied to certification

The provider must use case managers and supervisors who meet the qualifications in § 6.1:

- **Case managers** must meet one of the listed education/licensure pathways, such as certain master’s or bachelor’s degrees with specified related coursework/experience, or certain NC licensure categories with 2 years of human services experience. ([Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment), § 6.1.1)
- Case managers must be able to assess, create beneficiary-centered goals, make referrals, monitor care, and document accurately. (§ 6.1.2)
- Case managers must have documented HIV knowledge within **1 year of hire**, including transmission, treatment, medications, barriers to treatment compliance, and other listed skills and abilities. (§ 6.1.3)
- **Supervisors** must meet specified education/licensure and experience standards. (§ 6.1.4)
- Supervisors must be able to direct and evaluate case management quality and understand relevant federal and state assistance programs for people with HIV. (§ 6.1.5)

### Recertification requirements

For recertification, the provider must:

- submit a **complete and signed Recertification Application** within **60 calendar days of receipt**;
- submit copies of all items from the initial certification materials that have changed since initial certification;
- submit copies of all HIV case manager and supervisor credentials;
- submit annual summary or summaries of **quality improvement activities**, including outcome metrics; and
- submit to a **recertification on-site visit**, including review of beneficiary records or other clinical/business documentation as needed. ([Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment), § 6.2.4)

The recertification checklist also reflects required application content, including:
- one physical location per application;
- signed checklist and application materials;
- lists of all case managers and supervisors with employment dates;
- counties served;
- services provided;
- operating hours and emergency after-hours plan;
- accrediting agency name and dates, or an explanation;
- satisfaction survey frequency and how results are used; and
- numbers of Medicaid and Ryan White clients. ([dma-3157 HIV Case Management Provider Recertification Application Checklist](https://policies.ncdhhs.gov/wp-content/uploads/dma-3157.pdf))

## When a provider can be decertified

A provider may be decertified for any of the following:

- failure to provide core service components;
- fraudulent billing practices;
- owner conviction of a felony;
- failure to develop, submit, and implement a written **plan of correction** for unmet requirements within **30 calendar days**;
- falsification of records;
- violation of beneficiary confidentiality;
- employing staff who do not meet the policy’s staff criteria;
- failure of staff to complete mandatory basic training within **90 days of employment**;
- failure of staff to obtain required continuing education units;
- failure to provide supervision sufficient to meet beneficiaries’ needs;
- failure to submit required documentation on time;
- failure to maintain documentation sufficient to support billing;
- failure to implement and enforce a quality improvement program;
- failure to timely report agency changes that prevent NC Medicaid or its contractor from contacting the provider;
- failure to comply with applicable federal and state laws, regulations, the state reimbursement plan, and Medicaid policies;
- failure to enroll any beneficiaries within **4 months of certification**; or
- failure to achieve or maintain the certification requirements in § 6.2. ([Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment), § 6.2.5)

## Practical bottom line

To stay certified, an HIV case management provider must keep qualified staff and supervisors, maintain accreditation, complete recertification, cooperate with oversight, maintain a functioning quality improvement program, and keep records/documentation strong enough to support both service delivery and billing. The most explicit decertification triggers are compliance failures in staffing, supervision, training, documentation, billing integrity, confidentiality, correction of deficiencies, and maintenance of certification standards. ([Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment); [dma-3157 HIV Case Management Provider Recertification Application Checklist](https://policies.ncdhhs.gov/wp-content/uploads/dma-3157.pdf))

